.Dd October 18, 2026
.Dt VOCALISE 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm vocalise
.Nd speak text with OVHcloud Riva, or with piper and espeak-ng locally
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl Lpq
.Op Fl A Ar output
.Op Fl b Ar spec
.Op Fl E Ar engine
.Op Fl f Ar format
.Op Fl l Ar lang
.Op Fl m Ar model
.Op Fl o Ar file
.Op Fl r Ar rate
.Op Fl s Ar speed
.Op Fl v Ar voice
.Op Ar text ...
.Nm
.Fl D
.Op Fl q
.Op Fl E Ar engine
.Op Fl l Ar lang
.Nm
.Fl d
.Fl E Ar engine
.Op Fl q
.Op Fl l Ar lang
.Op Ar voice ...
.Nm
.Fl h
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
turns text into speech, and plays it or writes it as audio.
The text is the operands, joined by spaces, or stdin when there are
none or the one operand is
.Ql - ;
whitespace alone is refused.
.Pp
Three engines can speak it:
.Bl -tag -width espeak
.It Cm riva
The Riva voices of OVHcloud AI Endpoints, the best of the three, over
the network.
They speak English, German, Spanish and Italian, and nothing else:
OVHcloud publishes no French voice.
Each language is its own host,
.Ql https://<model>.$OVH_AI_ENDPOINTS_DOMAIN/api/v1/tts/text_to_audio .
Riva refuses a request whose normalised text is over 2000 characters,
and normalising expands the text, so a long one is split on sentence
boundaries, then clause, then word boundaries, into pieces of at most
1200 characters, fewer at a high sample rate or a slow speed, and the
audio of the pieces is joined.
A piece that is still too big, in text or in the audio it returns, is
split again at half the size.
Riva costs nothing in the OVHcloud catalogue; only its rate limit
applies.
.It Cm piper
Neural voices, offline, from the voices installed in
.Ev PIPER_VOICE_PATH .
Both generations of piper are recognised, the original binary and
piper1-gpl, whose options differ.
.It Cm espeak
Formant synthesis by
.Xr espeak-ng 1
.Pq or Xr espeak 1 ,
robotic but intelligible, in about a hundred languages.
.El
.Pp
.Fl E
names one of them, or a selector that tries them in an order and takes
the first that can speak the language:
.Bl -tag -width offline
.It Cm auto
riva, then piper, then espeak-ng: the best voice first.
.It Cm offline
piper, then espeak-ng, and riva only if neither knows the language: a
preference for staying local.
.It Cm local
piper, then espeak-ng, and failure rather than the network: the text
never leaves the machine.
.El
.Pp
A
.Fl v
naming a
.Pa .onnx
file means piper, under any selector.
.Pp
Every engine produces WAV.
Riva synthesises at the rate asked for, and its WAV header, which
always claims 16 kHz, is written afresh; the local engines keep their
own rate, and
.Xr ffmpeg 1
resamples them to
.Fl r ,
or, without ffmpeg, they keep it and say so when
.Fl r
was given.
Any format but WAV is then transcoded: by ffmpeg when it is installed,
otherwise by
.Xr lame 1 ,
.Xr oggenc 1 ,
.Xr opusenc 1
or
.Xr flac 1 .
.Pp
Without
.Fl o ,
the audio is played when stdout is a terminal, and written to stdout
otherwise.
On stderr,
.Nm
announces the length of the text, the duration of the audio, its rate
and format, and the engine and voice that spoke it.
.Ss Credentials
Riva's API token is the first found of:
.Bl -enum -compact
.It
.Ev OVH_AI_TOKEN ;
.It
.Ev OVH_AI_ENDPOINTS_API_KEY ,
the name OVHcloud's own documentation uses;
.It
the first line of the file
.Ev OVH_AI_TOKEN_FILE ;
.It
the password of the first
.Ev OVH_AI_AUTHINFO_HOST
entry in
.Pa ~/.authinfo ,
then in
.Pa ~/.netrc ,
where lines starting with
.Ql #
are skipped and a trailing backslash joins a line to the next;
.It
the
.Ql api_key
of the client named
.Ql OVH
in the
.Xr aichat 1
configuration
.Ev AICHAT_CONFIG .
.El
.Pp
One token serves every AI Endpoints model.
It is handed to
.Xr curl 1
in a configuration file inside the temporary directory, readable by its
owner alone, rather than on curl's command line, where
.Xr ps 1
would show it to every user.
A token holding a character outside a bearer token's alphabet
.Pq RFC 6750: letters, digits and Ql ._~+/=-
is refused rather than escaped.
The local engines need no token.
.Ss Downloading piper voices
.Fl D
lists the voices that can be downloaded for
.Fl l ,
as
.Ar engine Ns / Ns Ar voice ,
from the index of the piper voice collection
.Pq Ev PIPER_VOICE_URL ;
piper is the only engine with one.
.Fl d
installs the voices named into the first writable directory of
.Ev PIPER_VOICE_PATH ,
creating its first entry when none is, and speaks nothing.
A voice is named by its full key, as
.Fl D
prints it
.Pq Ql fr_FR-siwis-medium ,
or, within the language of
.Fl l ,
by the short
.Ql siwis-medium
or
.Ql siwis ;
a name that fits more than one voice is refused, with the voices it
fits.
With no voice named,
.Fl d
installs every voice of
.Fl l ,
or every voice the engine has when
.Fl l
is not given: gigabytes, so the size is announced first, and voices
already installed are skipped, which makes an interrupted run
resumable.
Each voice is its
.Pa .onnx
model and its
.Pa .onnx.json ,
checked against the md5 the index gives when
.Xr jq 1
is installed, and installed only once both have arrived.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width "-E engine"
.It Fl A Ar output
Where
.Fl p
sends the sound:
.Cm auto ,
the default,
.Cm sndio ,
.Cm oss ,
.Cm pulse
or
.Cm alsa .
.Cm auto
tries sndio, then oss, on
.Fx ,
and pulse, then alsa, on Linux.
It never tries pulse on
.Fx ,
where PulseAudio sits on top of OSS
with a mute of its own, so a muted sink swallows the sound while every
command reports success.
An output named is used or fails, with no fallback.
sndio plays with
.Xr aucat 1
and honours
.Ev AUDIODEVICE ,
oss plays to
.Ev VOCALISE_OSS_DEVICE ,
pulse with
.Xr paplay 1 ,
ffplay or cvlc, alsa with
.Xr aplay 1 ;
ffmpeg can drive any of them.
.It Fl b Ar spec
The encoder setting: a bitrate for the lossy formats, as
.Ql 128k
or
.Ql 128 ,
or the compression level 0 to 8 for flac.
Without it each encoder uses its own default, which is the safer
choice: libvorbis has no 128k mode at 22 kHz mono, and refuses rather
than clamping.
It has no effect on WAV, and says so.
.It Fl D
List the voices that can be downloaded for
.Fl l ,
and exit.
.Fl E
may be left out, or name piper or a selector; naming riva or espeak is
an error, since piper is the only engine with voices to download.
.It Fl d
Download the voices named, or every voice of the language, and exit
.Pq Sx Downloading piper voices .
.Fl E
must name one engine; a selector does not say which engine a voice is
for.
.It Fl E Ar engine
The engine:
.Cm auto ,
.Cm offline ,
.Cm local ,
.Cm riva ,
.Cm piper
or
.Cm espeak ;
by default
.Ev VOCALISE_ENGINE ,
or
.Cm auto .
.It Fl f Ar format
The format written:
.Ql wav ,
.Ql flac ,
.Ql mp3 ,
.Ql ogg ,
.Ql opus ,
or any other extension ffmpeg can mux.
Without it the format is the extension of
.Fl o ,
and WAV when there is none.
.It Fl h
Print the usage, with the voice path and where the credentials are
read from, and exit.
.It Fl L
List the voices the chosen engine has for
.Fl l ,
and exit.
.It Fl l Ar lang
The language, by default
.Ql en :
.Ql en ,
.Ql de ,
.Ql es
or
.Ql it
on riva, and anything the local engines know elsewhere, such as
.Ql fr ,
.Ql pt-br
or
.Ql nl .
.It Fl m Ar model
The Riva model, instead of the one for
.Fl l .
.It Fl o Ar file
Write the audio to
.Ar file ,
or to stdout when it is
.Ql - .
.It Fl p
Play the audio.
With
.Fl o
it is written too; what is played is the lossless audio, whatever
.Fl f
writes.
.It Fl q
Quiet: announce nothing on stderr.
.It Fl r Ar rate
The sample rate, in Hz, from 8000 to 48000; 22050 by default.
.It Fl s Ar speed
The speaking rate, as a multiplier
.Pq Ql 0.8
or a percentage
.Pq Ql 80% ,
from 25% to 250%: SSML prosody on riva, which therefore takes plain
text rather than markup of one's own, words per minute on espeak-ng,
and the length scale on piper, so the same
.Fl s
means the same everywhere.
.It Fl v Ar voice
The voice, as the engine names it.
On riva its language prefix is optional, so
.Ql Male-1
and
.Ql English-US.Male-1
are the same voice.
On espeak it is a voice such as
.Ql fr-be .
On piper it is a full key, the short
.Ql tom-medium
or
.Ql tom
within the language, or the path of a
.Pa .onnx
model.
.Fl L
lists them.
.El
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width OVH_AI_ENDPOINTS_API_KEY
.It Ev AICHAT_CONFIG
The aichat configuration a token may be read from; by default
.Pa ~/.config/aichat/config.yaml .
.It Ev AUDIODEVICE
The sndio device sound is played to.
.It Ev OVH_AI_AUTHINFO_HOST
The machine whose password is the token in
.Pa ~/.authinfo
and
.Pa ~/.netrc ;
by default
.Ql oai.$OVH_AI_ENDPOINTS_DOMAIN .
.It Ev OVH_AI_ENDPOINTS_API_KEY
The API token, when
.Ev OVH_AI_TOKEN
is not set.
.It Ev OVH_AI_ENDPOINTS_DOMAIN
The domain of AI Endpoints; by default
.Ql endpoints.kepler.ai.cloud.ovh.net .
.It Ev OVH_AI_TOKEN
The API token.
.It Ev OVH_AI_TOKEN_FILE
A file whose first line is the token; by default
.Pa ~/.config/ovhcloud/ai-token .
.It Ev OVH_AI_TTS_ENDPOINT
The Riva endpoint, instead of the one the model's host makes.
.It Ev PIPER_VOICE_PATH
The directories piper voices are looked for in, colon-separated, in
order of preference; by default
.Pa ~/.local/share/piper/voices ,
.Pa /usr/local/share/piper-voices
and
.Pa /usr/share/piper-voices .
.It Ev PIPER_VOICE_URL
The piper voice collection; by default
.Lk https://huggingface.co/rhasspy/piper-voices/resolve/main .
.It Ev TMPDIR
Where the temporary directory is made, instead of
.Pa /tmp .
.It Ev VOCALISE_AUDIO
The default of
.Fl A .
.It Ev VOCALISE_ENGINE
The default of
.Fl E ;
set to
.Cm local
in a shell profile, it keeps every text on the machine unless
.Fl E
says otherwise.
.It Ev VOCALISE_OSS_DEVICE
The OSS device; by default
.Pa /dev/dsp .
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa ~/.local/share/piper/voices/
The first directory of the default voice path, where
.Fl d
installs voices for its user.
.It Pa ~/.config/ovhcloud/ai-token
The token, on its first line.
.It Pa ~/.authinfo , Pa ~/.netrc
The token, as the password of the AI Endpoints host.
.It Pa ~/.config/aichat/config.yaml
The token, as the
.Ql api_key
of the client named
.Ql OVH .
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width Ds -compact
.It 0
The text was spoken, written, listed or downloaded.
.It 1
A failure: no engine can speak the language, an unknown voice, no
token, no encoder for the format, no working audio output, or an error
from the API, whose answer is shown on stderr.
.It 2
A usage error: an unknown engine or output, a speed, rate or
.Fl b
out of range or unreadable, or no text and stdin a terminal.
.It 130
Interrupted.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Say something, through the speakers:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ vocalise "The build is done."
.Ed
.Pp
A chapter in French, offline, as Opus, a little slower:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ vocalise -E local -l fr -s 0.9 -o chapitre.opus < chapitre.txt
.Ed
.Pp
The French piper voices there are, then one of them installed:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ vocalise -D -l fr
$ vocalise -d -E piper -l fr siwis-medium
.Ed
.Pp
A calm English Riva voice, to a 64 kbit/s MP3:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ vocalise -E riva -v Female-Calm -b 64k -o notice.mp3 "Doors closing."
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr curl 1 ,
.Xr espeak-ng 1 ,
.Xr ffmpeg 1 ,
.Xr piper 1 ,
.Xr transcribe 1
.Sh CAVEATS
The Linux audio outputs, pulse and alsa, are new and have not been
tried on real hardware.
.Pp
Riva's
.Fl s
wraps the text in SSML, so markup of one's own in the text is escaped
rather than read as SSML.
